  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to employ a genetic algorithm (GA) using a binary representation to explore the solution space more thoroughly, escaping local minima identified by previous optimization methods. The current approach relies too heavily on local search, which can get trapped in suboptimal regions. The binary representation enforces crisp transitions between 0 and 1, which helps avoid overlap better than continuous values. We address this with tournament selection, crossover, and mutation while maintaining the constraint that the integral is 1.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h: Binary sequence of length n_points with exactly n_points/2 ones\n- k: Discrete shift index\n- obj: Fitness value = max_k [np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\") * (2.0/n_points]\n\nObjective: Minimize obj\nConstraints: All h[i] in {0,1}, sum(h) = n_points/2\nRepresentation: Binary sequence of length n_points\n\nFitness calculation:\nobj = max_k_ ||h(x)(1-h(x+k))|| integrated over [0,2] using discrete convolution\nImplementation uses mode=\"full\" with scaling factor 2.0/n_points\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n```python\ndef run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, **kwargs):\n    np.random.seed(seed)\n    n_points = 360  # Start with parent resolution if possible\n    \n    # Get parent if available\n    if 'initial_h_values' in globals():\n        parent_values = initial_h_values\n        n_points_parent = parent_values.size\n        if n_points_parent == n_points:\n            # Use parent to initialize population\n            pass\n\n    def make_binary_vector(n, num_ones):\n        return np.sort(np.random.choice(n, num_ones, replace=False) <= np.random.choice(n, num_ones, replace=False), \n                       axis=None).astype(int)\n    \n    def compute_fitness(ind):\n        corr = np.correlate(ind, 1-ind, mode='full')\n        return np.max(corr) * (2.0/n_points)\n    \n    initial_population = [make_binary_vector(n_points, n_points//2) \n                         for _ in range(5)]  # Population size 5 for n=360\n    \n    pop_size = len(initial_population)\n    best_fitness = float('inf')\n    best_ind = None\n    \n    for _ in range(50):  # 50 generations\n        fitness_list = [compute_fitness(ind) for ind in initial_population]\n        \n        # Update best solution\n        for i, fit_val in enumerate(fitness_list):\n            if fit_val < best_fitness:\n                best_fitness = fit_val\n                best_ind = initial_population[i]\n        \n        # Tournament selection (k=3)\n        tournament = np.random.choice(pop_size, 3, replace=False)\n        parent1 = initial_population[tournament[0]]\n        parent2 = initial_population[tournament[1]]\n        \n        # Crossover (0.8 chance)\n        if np.random.rand() < 0.8:\n            crossover_point = np.random.randint(1, n_points)\n            child1 = np.concatenate([parent1[:crossover_point], parent2[crossover_point:]])\n            child2 = np.concatenate([parent2[:crossover_point], parent1[crossover_point:]])\n        else:\n            child1, child2 = parent1, parent2\n        \n        # Mutation (0.2 chance)\n        if np.random.rand() < 0.2:\n            try:\n                i, j = np.random.choice(n_points, 2)\n                child1[i], child1[j] = child1[j], child1[i]\n            except IndexError:\n                pass\n        \n        # Replace worst member\n        fitness_list = [compute_fitness(ind) for ind in initial_population]\n        replace_idx = np.argmax(fitness_list)\n        initial_population[replace_idx] = child1\n    \n    # Post-GA refinement\n    refined_h = initial_population[np.argmin([compute_fitness(ind) for ind in initial_population])]\n    refined_h = refined_h.astype(float)\n    diff = n_points/2.0 - np.sum(refined_h)\n    # Very basic adjustment - slight resampling to keep sum near target\n    if abs(diff) > 1e-4:\n        rand_idx = np.random.randint(0, n_points)\n        add_amount = min(diff, 1.0-refined_h[rand_idx]) if diff>0 else max(diff,-refined_h[rand_idx])\n        refined_h[rand_idx] += add_amount\n```\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n```\nVariation Strategy 1: Magnitude-based crossover\n- Probability 0.3 for crossover when the parent has high peaks in correlation\n- Implementation: Monitor cross-correlation at each crossover attempt\n\nVariation Strategy 2: Multi-resolution search\n- Start at n_points = 180 (faster)\n- Then 360, then 720 to final resolution\n- Time budget allocation: 50% GA, 30% refinement, 20% migration\n- Steps:\n  * Double resolution on 60-40% budget\n```\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nDominant cost: GA evaluations (O(n_points^2) per individual, n_points=720)\nTime estimate per n_points=360: ~2.5 seconds evaluation cost\nWith 10 individuals: 0.25-0.5 seconds per generation\nProgressive timeout: 500ms interval for 8 evaluations each time\n\nFail-safe fallback:\n- If stuck, restart with parent (if available)\n- Repair by Gaussian perturbation of peaks/valleys\n- Compute on n_points=360 fallback\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nOutput format: (h_values, C5_bound, n_points)\nDimensions: h_values is [0,1] floats\nIntegral check: \n  if abs(sum(h_values)*dx - 1) > 1e-4: adjust via resampling\nC5 calculation:\n  dx = 2.0/n_points\n  corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n  C5 = max(corr) * dx\n\nIntegration of safety:\n- Safety checks on array sizes and values\n- Delta-based termination if no change in 10 generations\n- Repair routines to maintain constraints",
